<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayri Túpac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1535–1561</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sayri Túpac was ruler of the Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> State at Vilcabamba from 1545, after the death of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a>. In 1558 he accepted Spanish terms, left Vilcabamba and received estates in the Yucay valley in exchange for giving up his claims. He died in 1561, and his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titu-cusi-yupanqui">Titu Cusi Yupanqui</a> continued the resistance from Vilcabamba.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayri-tupac.jpg" alt="Sayri Túpac" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SAHUARAURA_p115_-_SAYRI_TTUPAC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
